= Localhost dashboard
On the repo you'll find a folder: `dashboard`.
This folder contains a python script that emulates a SCADA dashboard.
You will use this dashboard all along the lab, the first step is to run it locally on your laptop and analyse the traffic with wireshark.
You must analyse the http traffic generated by this dashboard.

#task[Run and analyse dashboard locally][
  Write a small report with basic information as capture / display filter you used and what you found on packet.
  You can illustrate with wireshark screenshots and concise explanation. Once polling is started in the web page, your browser and the dashboard exchange http traffic on port #traffic-port-http. In this task, we focus on the content of the payload.

  #subtask[
    On PyCharm, open the `main.py` file in the `dashboard` folder and run it.
    Open http://localhost:8050 in your browser. In the "HTTP Traffic Generator" tab, click "Start HTTP Polling" to generate traffic.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Open Wireshark and select the loopback / localhost interface. Choose the right capture filter.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Select specific display filter to get only the `http` traffic and show in your report a packet in depth from the header to the json payload
  ]

]

#pagebreak()

= IIoT Rack and LAN
From this task on, we will use the IIoT (Industrial Internet of Things) Rack.
This rack (@fig:iiot-rack:hardware) contains several devices we will see later. We are now focusing on the Edge Router on top right.
You will connect your laptop to this router, experiment with DHCP and analyse local traffic.

The router is the default gateway of this LAN. You can find its address in your network settings and open it in a browser.

#figure(
  image("../res/img/iiot-rack-hardware.svg", width: 60%),
  caption: [IIoT Rack - hardware]
) <fig:iiot-rack:hardware>

#task[LAN connection][
  You previously ran the dashboard locally and connected to it with the same computer.
  You will now exchange with a partner.
  One student will be the server and another the client.
  You must observe the difference with the previous task and write it down in your report.

  #subtask[
    Connect both your laptops to the edge router with an Ethernet cable and turn off Wi-Fi for the rest of the lab. You will receive an IPv4 address by DHCP.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Find your IP address on this LAN. Explain in your report which method you used. \
    // Tips: One method is to use `ipconfig` for Windows, `ifconfig` on macOS, `ip a` on Linux.
  ]

  #subtask[
    One student starts the python script, the other opens `http://<IP of the server>:8050` in a browser and starts the HTTP polling as in the previous task.
    Analyse the traffic with Wireshark on both computers. You need to modify the interface on Wireshark.
    Show and explain the main difference with the previous task in your report.

    #warningbox[
      Maybe you will have some issues to communicate with another laptop, especially on Windows. If you experience any issues, don't hesitate to ask for help. It can be caused by an aggressive antivirus.
    ]

  ]

]
#bonus[Lease time][
  Find your lease time. Show and explain in your report how you found this info. Explain what a lease is and the consequences of a shorter / longer lease.
  _Hint: look at `ipconfig /all` (Windows), `ipconfig getpacket <interface>` (macOS) or the DHCP option "IP Address Lease Time" in Wireshark._
]

= Modbus traffic

#task[Analyse Modbus traffic][
  The dashboard you used previously also generates modbus traffic on port #traffic-port-modbus. You will now analyse this traffic with Wireshark.

  #figure([
    #modbus-setup-fig
    ],
    caption: [Modbus LAN setup - server and client connected through the edge router]
  ) <fig:modbus:setup>

  #subtask[
    Keep both laptops connected to the Edge Router with the dashboard running on both of your laptops.
    One laptop acts as *Modbus Server* and one as *Modbus Client*.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Open the dashboard of the client laptop.
    In the "Modbus TCP / SCADA" tab, enter the *IP address of the server laptop* and port #traffic-port-modbus, then click "Apply Target".
    Select "FC 01 — Read Coils" as Query Target and click "Send Query" (or "Resume Master Poller").
  ]

  #subtask[
    Capture the traffic on the client laptop.
    Select specific display filter to get only the Modbus traffic and show in your report a query and response of a coil read.
  ]
]

= Dashboard on Raspberry Pi
On the IIoT Rack (@fig:iiot-rack:hardware) you also have a Raspberry Pi at the bottom.
This Raspberry Pi already runs another dashboard.
You will now map the MAC address of the Raspberry Pi to a fixed IP address on the router.
You will then access the dashboard on the Raspberry Pi with this IP and a name.

#task[Map RPI MAC to IP][
  Your Raspberry Pi currently gets its IP from the DHCP server on the router.
  You will now map the MAC address of the Raspberry Pi to #raspberry-pi-ip.

  #subtask[
    To log in to the router with these credentials:
    - user: #iiot-rack-router-user
    - password: #iiot-rack-router-pwd
  ]

  #subtask[
    Find the MAC address of the Raspberry Pi and its current IP address.
    Set #raspberry-pi-ip for the Raspberry Pi through a static DHCP mapping on the router.
    #infobox[
      The menu in the Ubiquiti router can be found under:\
      `Services` $->$ `DHCP Server` $->$ `Configure Static Map`
      ]
    The Raspberry Pi only gets its new address when it renews its lease: unplug and replug its Ethernet cable (or reboot it).
  ]

  #subtask[
    Open `http://192.168.1.4:8050` in your browser and show with Wireshark the `http` traffic between your laptop and the Raspberry Pi.
  ]
]

#task[DNS for webpage on RPI][
  Since you now have a fixed IP address for the Raspberry Pi, you can access it with a name. We have already configured a DNS server on the router with a hostname for #raspberry-pi-ip. You can now access the dashboard on the Raspberry Pi with the name #raspberry-pi-dns-name. You will now analyse the traffic with Wireshark and write a small report about it.

  #subtask[
    Verify you have access to the dashboard as before but now with #raspberry-pi-dns-name.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Observe the DNS resolution and the traffic with Wireshark. Show and explain what you see in your own words in your report.
    #infobox[
      You can force nslookup to use a specific DNS server and bypass your cache: \
      e.g.: `nslookup dashboard.lan 192.168.1.1` uses the DNS server `192.168.1.1` to resolve `dashboard.lan`.
    ]
  ]
]
